                text: "When we think about water consumption, we usually picture the tap water we use to brush our teeth, take a shower, or wash our dishes. However, scientists urge us to look into our \"water footprint,\" a broader concept which measures both direct and indirect water use. Indirect water use refers to the massive, often invisible amount of fresh water required to produce the goods we buy and consume daily. For instance, agriculture is highly water-intensive; growing the cotton needed for a single t-shirt can consume over 2,500 liters of water. Similarly, producing a single kilogram of beef requires approximately 15,000 liters. Because our freshwater resources are incredibly precious, limited, and unevenly distributed globally, we must change our shopping and dietary habits. Environmentalists suggest that we shouldn't ignore this hidden waste any longer. We had better start buying second-hand clothing, eating more plant-based meals, and actively reducing our overall consumption. By becoming conscious consumers, we can significantly lower our water footprint. If we refuse to adapt, we might eventually run out of fresh water for future generations.",
